{"id":"msg-1779415593506-3fd9ee","type":"tool","content":"     1→# Skills Configuration Guide\n     2→\n     3→This guide explains how to create and configure skills in Incaier Agent.\n     4→\n     5→## What Are Skills?\n     6→\n     7→Skills are specialized instructions that extend Claude's capabilities for specific tasks. They use **the exact same SKILL.md format as the Claude Code SDK** - making skills fully compatible between systems.\n     8→\n     9→**Key points:**\n    10→- Skills are invoked via slash commands (e.g., `/commit`, `/review-pr`)\n    11→- Skills can be automatically triggered by file patterns (globs)\n    12→- Skills can pre-approve specific tools to run without prompting\n    13→- The SKILL.md format is identical to what Claude Code uses internally\n    14→\n    15→## Same Format as Claude Code SDK\n    16→\n    17→Incaier Agent uses **the identical SKILL.md format** as the Claude Code SDK. This means:\n    18→\n    19→1. **Format compatibility**: Any skill written for Claude Code works in Incaier Agent\n    20→2. **Same frontmatter fields**: `name`, `description`, `globs`, `alwaysAllow`, `requiredSources`\n    21→3. **Same content structure**: Markdown body with instructions for Claude\n    22→\n    23→**What Incaier Agent adds:**\n    24→- **Visual icons**: Display custom icons in the UI for each skill\n    25→- **Workspace organization**: Skills are scoped to workspaces\n    26→- **UI management**: Browse, edit, and validate skills through the interface\n    27→\n    28→## Skill Precedence\n    29→\n    30→When a skill is invoked (e.g., `/commit`):\n    31→\n    32→1. **Workspace skill checked first** - If `~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{id}/skills/commit/SKILL.md` exists, it's used\n    33→2. **SDK skill as fallback** - If no workspace skill exists, the built-in SDK skill is used\n    34→\n    35→This allows you to:\n    36→- **Override SDK skills** - Create a workspace skill with the same slug to replace built-in behavior\n    37→- **Extend SDK skills** - Reference SDK behavior in your custom skill and add workspace-specific instructions\n    38→- **Create new skills** - Add entirely new skills not in the SDK\n    39→\n    40→## Skill Storage\n    41→\n    42→Skills are stored as folders:\n    43→```\n    44→~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{workspaceId}/skills/{slug}/\n    45→├── SKILL.md          # Required: Skill definition (same format as Claude Code SDK)\n    46→├── icon.svg          # Recommended: Skill icon for UI display\n    47→├── icon.png          # Alternative: PNG icon\n    48→└── (other files)     # Optional: Additional resources\n    49→```\n    50→\n    51→## SKILL.md Format\n    52→\n    53→The format is identical to Claude Code SDK skills:\n    54→\n    55→```yaml\n    56→---\n    57→name: \"Skill Display Name\"\n    58→description: \"Brief description shown in skill list\"\n    59→globs: [\"*.ts\", \"*.tsx\"]     # Optional: file patterns that trigger skill\n    60→alwaysAllow: [\"Bash\"]        # Optional: tools to always allow\n    61→requiredSources:             # Optional: sources to auto-enable on invocation\n    62→  - linear\n    63→---\n    64→\n    65→# Skill Instructions\n    66→\n    67→Your skill content goes here. This is injected into Claude's context\n    68→when the skill is active.\n    69→\n    70→## Guidelines\n    71→\n    72→- Specific instructions for Claude\n    73→- Best practices to follow\n    74→- Things to avoid\n    75→\n    76→## Examples\n    77→\n    78→Show Claude how to perform the task correctly.\n    79→```\n    80→\n    81→## Metadata Fields\n    82→\n    83→### name (required)\n    84→Display name for the skill. Shown in the UI and skill list.\n    85→\n    86→### description (required)\n    87→Brief description (1-2 sentences) explaining what the skill does.\n    88→\n    89→### globs (optional)\n    90→Array of glob patterns. When a file matching these patterns is being worked on,\n    91→the skill may be automatically suggested or activated.\n    92→\n    93→```yaml\n    94→globs:\n    95→  - \"*.test.ts\"           # Test files\n    96→  - \"*.spec.tsx\"          # React test files\n    97→  - \"**/__tests__/**\"     # Test directories\n    98→```\n    99→\n   100→### alwaysAllow (optional)\n   101→Array of tool names that are automatically allowed when this skill is active.\n   102→Useful for skills that require specific tools without prompting.\n   103→\n   104→```yaml\n   105→alwaysAllow:\n   106→  - \"Bash\"                # Allow bash commands\n   107→  - \"Write\"               # Allow file writes\n   108→```\n   109→\n   110→### requiredSources (optional)\n   111→Array of source slugs to auto-enable when this skill is invoked.\n   112→When a user mentions the skill, the listed sources are enabled for the session\n   113→before the agent starts — so tools from those sources are available from the first turn.\n   114→\n   115→Sources must exist in the workspace and be authenticated. Unauthenticated or\n   116→missing sources are silently skipped (the existing runtime auto-enable handles them\n   117→as a fallback).\n   118→\n   119→```yaml\n   120→requiredSources:\n   121→  - linear               # Auto-enable Linear source\n   122→  - github               # Auto-enable GitHub source\n   123→```\n   124→\n   125→## Creating a Skill\n   126→\n   127→### 1. Create the skill directory\n   128→\n   129→```bash\n   130→mkdir -p ~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{ws}/skills/my-skill\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→### 2. Write SKILL.md\n   134→\n   135→```markdown\n   136→---\n   137→name: \"Code Review\"\n   138→description: \"Review code changes for quality, security, and best practices\"\n   139→globs: [\"*.ts\", \"*.tsx\", \"*.js\", \"*.jsx\"]\n   140→---\n   141→\n   142→# Code Review Skill\n   143→\n   144→When reviewing code, focus on:\n   145→\n   146→## Quality Checks\n   147→- Consistent code style\n   148→- Clear naming conventions\n   149→- Appropriate abstractions\n   150→\n   151→## Security Checks\n   152→- Input validation\n   153→- Authentication/authorization\n   154→- Sensitive data handling\n   155→\n   156→## Best Practices\n   157→- Error handling\n   158→- Performance considerations\n   159→- Test coverage\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→### 3. Add an icon (IMPORTANT)\n   163→\n   164→Every skill should have a visually relevant icon. This helps users quickly identify skills in the UI.\n   165→\n   166→**Icon requirements:**\n   167→- **Filename**: Must be `icon.svg`, `icon.png`, `icon.jpg`, or `icon.jpeg`\n   168→- **Format**: SVG preferred (scalable, crisp at all sizes)\n   169→- **Size**: For PNG/JPG, use at least 64x64 pixels\n   170→\n   171→**How to get an icon:**\n   172→\n   173→1. **Search online icon libraries:**\n   174→   - [Heroicons](https://heroicons.com/) - MIT licensed\n   175→   - [Feather Icons](https://feathericons.com/) - MIT licensed\n   176→   - [Simple Icons](https://simpleicons.org/) - Brand icons (git, npm, etc.)\n   177→\n   178→2. **Use WebFetch to download:**\n   179→   ```\n   180→   # Find an appropriate icon URL and download it\n   181→   WebFetch to get SVG content, then save to icon.svg\n   182→   ```\n   183→\n   184→3. **Match the skill's purpose:**\n   185→   - Git/commit skill → git icon or commit icon\n   186→   - Test skill → checkmark or test tube icon\n   187→   - Deploy skill → rocket or cloud icon\n   188→   - Review skill → magnifying glass or eye icon\n   189→\n   190→### 4. Validate the skill\n   191→\n   192→**IMPORTANT**: Always validate after creating or editing a skill:\n   193→\n   194→```\n   195→skill_validate({ skillSlug: \"my-skill\" })\n   196→```\n   197→\n   198→This validates:\n   199→- Slug format (lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphens only)\n   200→- SKILL.md exists and is readable\n   201→- YAML frontmatter is valid\n   202→- Required fields present (name, description)\n   203→- Content is non-empty\n   204→- Icon format (if present)\n   205→\n   206→## Example Skills\n   207→\n   208→### Commit Message Skill\n   209→\n   210→```yaml\n   211→---\n   212→name: \"Commit\"\n   213→description: \"Create well-formatted git commit messages\"\n   214→alwaysAllow: [\"Bash\"]\n   215→---\n   216→\n   217→# Commit Message Guidelines\n   218→\n   219→When creating commits:\n   220→\n   221→1. **Format**: Use conventional commits\n   222→   - `feat:` New feature\n   223→   - `fix:` Bug fix\n   224→   - `docs:` Documentation\n   225→   - `refactor:` Code refactoring\n   226→   - `test:` Adding tests\n   227→\n   228→2. **Style**:\n   229→   - Keep subject line under 72 characters\n   230→   - Use imperative mood (\"Add feature\" not \"Added feature\")\n   231→   - Explain why, not what (the diff shows what)\n   232→\n   233→3. **Co-authorship**:\n   234→   Always include: `Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`\n   235→```\n   236→\n   237→**Recommended icon**: Git commit icon from Heroicons or Simple Icons\n   238→\n   239→### Team Standards Skill\n   240→\n   241→```yaml\n   242→---\n   243→name: \"Team Standards\"\n   244→description: \"Enforce team coding conventions and patterns\"\n   245→globs: [\"src/**/*.ts\", \"src/**/*.tsx\"]\n   246→---\n   247→\n   248→# Team Coding Standards\n   249→\n   250→## File Organization\n   251→- One component per file\n   252→- Co-locate tests with source files\n   253→- Use barrel exports (index.ts)\n   254→\n   255→## Naming Conventions\n   256→- Components: PascalCase\n   257→- Hooks: camelCase with `use` prefix\n   258→- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE\n   259→\n   260→## Import Order\n   261→1. External packages\n   262→2. Internal packages (@company/*)\n   263→3. Relative imports\n   264→```\n   265→\n   266→**Recommended icon**: Clipboard list or checklist icon\n   267→\n   268→### Skill with Required Sources\n   269→\n   270→```yaml\n   271→---\n   272→name: \"Linear Triage\"\n   273→description: \"Triage and prioritize Linear issues\"\n   274→requiredSources:\n   275→  - linear\n   276→---\n   277→\n   278→# Linear Triage\n   279→\n   280→When triaging issues:\n   281→1. List unassigned issues from the current sprint\n   282→2. Categorize by severity\n   283→3. Suggest assignees based on expertise\n   284→```\n   285→\n   286→**Recommended icon**: Kanban board or list icon\n   287→\n   288→When this skill is invoked, the `linear` source is automatically enabled for the\n   289→session — no manual toggle needed.\n   290→\n   291→## Overriding SDK Skills\n   292→\n   293→To customize a built-in SDK skill like `/commit`:\n   294→\n   295→1. Create `~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{ws}/skills/commit/SKILL.md`\n   296→2. Write your custom instructions\n   297→3. Add an icon\n   298→4. Run `skill_validate({ skillSlug: \"commit\" })`\n   299→\n   300→Your skill will be used instead of the SDK's built-in version.\n   301→\n   302→This is useful for:\n   303→- Adding team-specific commit message formats\n   304→- Enforcing project-specific coding standards\n   305→- Customizing review criteria for your codebase\n   306→\n   307→## Best Practices\n   308→\n   309→1. **Be specific**: Give Claude clear, actionable instructions\n   310→2. **Include examples**: Show the expected output format\n   311→3. **Set boundaries**: Explain what NOT to do\n   312→4. **Keep focused**: One skill = one specific task or domain\n   313→5. **Add a relevant icon**: Makes skills easily identifiable in the UI\n   314→6. **Always validate**: Run `skill_validate` after creating or editing\n   315→\n   316→## Troubleshooting\n   317→\n   318→**Skill not loading:**\n   319→- Check slug format (lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphens only)\n   320→- Verify SKILL.md exists and is readable\n   321→- Run `skill_validate` for detailed errors\n   322→\n   323→**Skill not triggering:**\n   324→- Check glob patterns match your files\n   325→- Verify skill is in correct workspace\n   326→\n   327→**Icon not showing:**\n   328→- Use supported formats: svg, png, jpg, jpeg\n   329→- File must be named `icon.{ext}` (not `my-icon.svg`)\n   330→- Check icon file is not corrupted\n   331→- For SVG, ensure valid XML structure\n   332→","timestamp":1779415593506,"toolName":"Read","toolUseId":"toolu_05f00e4bbeb746f4bfce3776","toolInput":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\26011970\\.incaier-agent\\docs\\skills.md"},"toolResult":"     1→# Skills Configuration Guide\n     2→\n     3→This guide explains how to create and configure skills in Incaier Agent.\n     4→\n     5→## What Are Skills?\n     6→\n     7→Skills are specialized instructions that extend Claude's capabilities for specific tasks. They use **the exact same SKILL.md format as the Claude Code SDK** - making skills fully compatible between systems.\n     8→\n     9→**Key points:**\n    10→- Skills are invoked via slash commands (e.g., `/commit`, `/review-pr`)\n    11→- Skills can be automatically triggered by file patterns (globs)\n    12→- Skills can pre-approve specific tools to run without prompting\n    13→- The SKILL.md format is identical to what Claude Code uses internally\n    14→\n    15→## Same Format as Claude Code SDK\n    16→\n    17→Incaier Agent uses **the identical SKILL.md format** as the Claude Code SDK. This means:\n    18→\n    19→1. **Format compatibility**: Any skill written for Claude Code works in Incaier Agent\n    20→2. **Same frontmatter fields**: `name`, `description`, `globs`, `alwaysAllow`, `requiredSources`\n    21→3. **Same content structure**: Markdown body with instructions for Claude\n    22→\n    23→**What Incaier Agent adds:**\n    24→- **Visual icons**: Display custom icons in the UI for each skill\n    25→- **Workspace organization**: Skills are scoped to workspaces\n    26→- **UI management**: Browse, edit, and validate skills through the interface\n    27→\n    28→## Skill Precedence\n    29→\n    30→When a skill is invoked (e.g., `/commit`):\n    31→\n    32→1. **Workspace skill checked first** - If `~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{id}/skills/commit/SKILL.md` exists, it's used\n    33→2. **SDK skill as fallback** - If no workspace skill exists, the built-in SDK skill is used\n    34→\n    35→This allows you to:\n    36→- **Override SDK skills** - Create a workspace skill with the same slug to replace built-in behavior\n    37→- **Extend SDK skills** - Reference SDK behavior in your custom skill and add workspace-specific instructions\n    38→- **Create new skills** - Add entirely new skills not in the SDK\n    39→\n    40→## Skill Storage\n    41→\n    42→Skills are stored as folders:\n    43→```\n    44→~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{workspaceId}/skills/{slug}/\n    45→├── SKILL.md          # Required: Skill definition (same format as Claude Code SDK)\n    46→├── icon.svg          # Recommended: Skill icon for UI display\n    47→├── icon.png          # Alternative: PNG icon\n    48→└── (other files)     # Optional: Additional resources\n    49→```\n    50→\n    51→## SKILL.md Format\n    52→\n    53→The format is identical to Claude Code SDK skills:\n    54→\n    55→```yaml\n    56→---\n    57→name: \"Skill Display Name\"\n    58→description: \"Brief description shown in skill list\"\n    59→globs: [\"*.ts\", \"*.tsx\"]     # Optional: file patterns that trigger skill\n    60→alwaysAllow: [\"Bash\"]        # Optional: tools to always allow\n    61→requiredSources:             # Optional: sources to auto-enable on invocation\n    62→  - linear\n    63→---\n    64→\n    65→# Skill Instructions\n    66→\n    67→Your skill content goes here. This is injected into Claude's context\n    68→when the skill is active.\n    69→\n    70→## Guidelines\n    71→\n    72→- Specific instructions for Claude\n    73→- Best practices to follow\n    74→- Things to avoid\n    75→\n    76→## Examples\n    77→\n    78→Show Claude how to perform the task correctly.\n    79→```\n    80→\n    81→## Metadata Fields\n    82→\n    83→### name (required)\n    84→Display name for the skill. Shown in the UI and skill list.\n    85→\n    86→### description (required)\n    87→Brief description (1-2 sentences) explaining what the skill does.\n    88→\n    89→### globs (optional)\n    90→Array of glob patterns. When a file matching these patterns is being worked on,\n    91→the skill may be automatically suggested or activated.\n    92→\n    93→```yaml\n    94→globs:\n    95→  - \"*.test.ts\"           # Test files\n    96→  - \"*.spec.tsx\"          # React test files\n    97→  - \"**/__tests__/**\"     # Test directories\n    98→```\n    99→\n   100→### alwaysAllow (optional)\n   101→Array of tool names that are automatically allowed when this skill is active.\n   102→Useful for skills that require specific tools without prompting.\n   103→\n   104→```yaml\n   105→alwaysAllow:\n   106→  - \"Bash\"                # Allow bash commands\n   107→  - \"Write\"               # Allow file writes\n   108→```\n   109→\n   110→### requiredSources (optional)\n   111→Array of source slugs to auto-enable when this skill is invoked.\n   112→When a user mentions the skill, the listed sources are enabled for the session\n   113→before the agent starts — so tools from those sources are available from the first turn.\n   114→\n   115→Sources must exist in the workspace and be authenticated. Unauthenticated or\n   116→missing sources are silently skipped (the existing runtime auto-enable handles them\n   117→as a fallback).\n   118→\n   119→```yaml\n   120→requiredSources:\n   121→  - linear               # Auto-enable Linear source\n   122→  - github               # Auto-enable GitHub source\n   123→```\n   124→\n   125→## Creating a Skill\n   126→\n   127→### 1. Create the skill directory\n   128→\n   129→```bash\n   130→mkdir -p ~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{ws}/skills/my-skill\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→### 2. Write SKILL.md\n   134→\n   135→```markdown\n   136→---\n   137→name: \"Code Review\"\n   138→description: \"Review code changes for quality, security, and best practices\"\n   139→globs: [\"*.ts\", \"*.tsx\", \"*.js\", \"*.jsx\"]\n   140→---\n   141→\n   142→# Code Review Skill\n   143→\n   144→When reviewing code, focus on:\n   145→\n   146→## Quality Checks\n   147→- Consistent code style\n   148→- Clear naming conventions\n   149→- Appropriate abstractions\n   150→\n   151→## Security Checks\n   152→- Input validation\n   153→- Authentication/authorization\n   154→- Sensitive data handling\n   155→\n   156→## Best Practices\n   157→- Error handling\n   158→- Performance considerations\n   159→- Test coverage\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→### 3. Add an icon (IMPORTANT)\n   163→\n   164→Every skill should have a visually relevant icon. This helps users quickly identify skills in the UI.\n   165→\n   166→**Icon requirements:**\n   167→- **Filename**: Must be `icon.svg`, `icon.png`, `icon.jpg`, or `icon.jpeg`\n   168→- **Format**: SVG preferred (scalable, crisp at all sizes)\n   169→- **Size**: For PNG/JPG, use at least 64x64 pixels\n   170→\n   171→**How to get an icon:**\n   172→\n   173→1. **Search online icon libraries:**\n   174→   - [Heroicons](https://heroicons.com/) - MIT licensed\n   175→   - [Feather Icons](https://feathericons.com/) - MIT licensed\n   176→   - [Simple Icons](https://simpleicons.org/) - Brand icons (git, npm, etc.)\n   177→\n   178→2. **Use WebFetch to download:**\n   179→   ```\n   180→   # Find an appropriate icon URL and download it\n   181→   WebFetch to get SVG content, then save to icon.svg\n   182→   ```\n   183→\n   184→3. **Match the skill's purpose:**\n   185→   - Git/commit skill → git icon or commit icon\n   186→   - Test skill → checkmark or test tube icon\n   187→   - Deploy skill → rocket or cloud icon\n   188→   - Review skill → magnifying glass or eye icon\n   189→\n   190→### 4. Validate the skill\n   191→\n   192→**IMPORTANT**: Always validate after creating or editing a skill:\n   193→\n   194→```\n   195→skill_validate({ skillSlug: \"my-skill\" })\n   196→```\n   197→\n   198→This validates:\n   199→- Slug format (lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphens only)\n   200→- SKILL.md exists and is readable\n   201→- YAML frontmatter is valid\n   202→- Required fields present (name, description)\n   203→- Content is non-empty\n   204→- Icon format (if present)\n   205→\n   206→## Example Skills\n   207→\n   208→### Commit Message Skill\n   209→\n   210→```yaml\n   211→---\n   212→name: \"Commit\"\n   213→description: \"Create well-formatted git commit messages\"\n   214→alwaysAllow: [\"Bash\"]\n   215→---\n   216→\n   217→# Commit Message Guidelines\n   218→\n   219→When creating commits:\n   220→\n   221→1. **Format**: Use conventional commits\n   222→   - `feat:` New feature\n   223→   - `fix:` Bug fix\n   224→   - `docs:` Documentation\n   225→   - `refactor:` Code refactoring\n   226→   - `test:` Adding tests\n   227→\n   228→2. **Style**:\n   229→   - Keep subject line under 72 characters\n   230→   - Use imperative mood (\"Add feature\" not \"Added feature\")\n   231→   - Explain why, not what (the diff shows what)\n   232→\n   233→3. **Co-authorship**:\n   234→   Always include: `Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`\n   235→```\n   236→\n   237→**Recommended icon**: Git commit icon from Heroicons or Simple Icons\n   238→\n   239→### Team Standards Skill\n   240→\n   241→```yaml\n   242→---\n   243→name: \"Team Standards\"\n   244→description: \"Enforce team coding conventions and patterns\"\n   245→globs: [\"src/**/*.ts\", \"src/**/*.tsx\"]\n   246→---\n   247→\n   248→# Team Coding Standards\n   249→\n   250→## File Organization\n   251→- One component per file\n   252→- Co-locate tests with source files\n   253→- Use barrel exports (index.ts)\n   254→\n   255→## Naming Conventions\n   256→- Components: PascalCase\n   257→- Hooks: camelCase with `use` prefix\n   258→- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE\n   259→\n   260→## Import Order\n   261→1. External packages\n   262→2. Internal packages (@company/*)\n   263→3. Relative imports\n   264→```\n   265→\n   266→**Recommended icon**: Clipboard list or checklist icon\n   267→\n   268→### Skill with Required Sources\n   269→\n   270→```yaml\n   271→---\n   272→name: \"Linear Triage\"\n   273→description: \"Triage and prioritize Linear issues\"\n   274→requiredSources:\n   275→  - linear\n   276→---\n   277→\n   278→# Linear Triage\n   279→\n   280→When triaging issues:\n   281→1. List unassigned issues from the current sprint\n   282→2. Categorize by severity\n   283→3. Suggest assignees based on expertise\n   284→```\n   285→\n   286→**Recommended icon**: Kanban board or list icon\n   287→\n   288→When this skill is invoked, the `linear` source is automatically enabled for the\n   289→session — no manual toggle needed.\n   290→\n   291→## Overriding SDK Skills\n   292→\n   293→To customize a built-in SDK skill like `/commit`:\n   294→\n   295→1. Create `~/.incaier-agent/workspaces/{ws}/skills/commit/SKILL.md`\n   296→2. Write your custom instructions\n   297→3. Add an icon\n   298→4. Run `skill_validate({ skillSlug: \"commit\" })`\n   299→\n   300→Your skill will be used instead of the SDK's built-in version.\n   301→\n   302→This is useful for:\n   303→- Adding team-specific commit message formats\n   304→- Enforcing project-specific coding standards\n   305→- Customizing review criteria for your codebase\n   306→\n   307→## Best Practices\n   308→\n   309→1. **Be specific**: Give Claude clear, actionable instructions\n   310→2. **Include examples**: Show the expected output format\n   311→3. **Set boundaries**: Explain what NOT to do\n   312→4. **Keep focused**: One skill = one specific task or domain\n   313→5. **Add a relevant icon**: Makes skills easily identifiable in the UI\n   314→6. **Always validate**: Run `skill_validate` after creating or editing\n   315→\n   316→## Troubleshooting\n   317→\n   318→**Skill not loading:**\n   319→- Check slug format (lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphens only)\n   320→- Verify SKILL.md exists and is readable\n   321→- Run `skill_validate` for detailed errors\n   322→\n   323→**Skill not triggering:**\n   324→- Check glob patterns match your files\n   325→- Verify skill is in correct workspace\n   326→\n   327→**Icon not showing:**\n   328→- Use supported formats: svg, png, jpg, jpeg\n   329→- File must be named `icon.{ext}` (not `my-icon.svg`)\n   330→- Check icon file is not corrupted\n   331→- For SVG, ensure valid XML structure\n   332→","toolStatus":"completed","toolDisplayMeta":{"displayName":"读取","category":"native"},"isError":false,"turnId":"msg_47eae0ff-399a-497b-8d6a-ff995fa10a01"}
